From: Tim Douglas <me@timdoug.com>
Subject: [PATCH] openvt: switch back after -w through a fresh VT fd

With -s -w, openvt waits for its child and then switches back to the VT
it started on through fd 0, the new VT.  But the child is a session
leader with that VT as its controlling tty, so its exit hangs the VT up,
and every ioctl on openvt's fds to it fails with EIO: VT_ACTIVATE
failed, openvt exited 1 (its message on the hung-up VT, unseen), and the
new VT stayed in front with nothing on it.  On the WikiReader that left
the panel off, since the console only takes it back with its VT.  Close
fds 0-2, which also lets VT_DISALLOCATE succeed, and switch back through
/dev/tty0, the current VT, as kbd's openvt opens a console fd for it.

Signed-off-by: Tim Douglas <me@timdoug.com>
Upstream: not submitted

--- a/console-tools/openvt.c	2026-11-06 16:39:52
+++ b/console-tools/openvt.c	2026-11-06 16:39:52
@@ -64,7 +64,6 @@
 -f: always in effect
 -l: not implemented, ignored
 -v: ignored
--ws: does NOT switch back
 */
 
 /* Helper: does this fd understand VT_xxx? */
@@ -187,11 +186,25 @@
 		/* We have only one child, wait for it */
 		safe_waitpid(-1, NULL, 0); /* loops on EINTR */
 		if (flags & OPT_s) {
-			console_make_active(STDIN_FILENO, vtstat.v_active);
+			int fd;
+
+			/* The child was a session leader with the new VT as
+			 * its controlling tty, so its exit hung the VT up:
+			 * every ioctl on our fds 0-2 now fails with EIO.  Let
+			 * go of them, which also lets the VT be deallocated,
+			 * and switch back through the current VT, as kbd's
+			 * openvt does. */
+			close(STDIN_FILENO);
+			close(STDOUT_FILENO);
+			close(STDERR_FILENO);
+			fd = open("/dev/tty0", O_RDWR);
+			if (fd < 0 || not_vt_fd(fd))
+				fd = get_vt_fd();
+			console_make_active(fd, vtstat.v_active);
 			// Compat: even with -c N (try to) disallocate:
 			// # /usr/app/kbd-1.12/bin/openvt -f -c 9 -ws sleep 5
 			// openvt: could not deallocate console 9
-			xioctl(STDIN_FILENO, VT_DISALLOCATE, (void*)(ptrdiff_t)vtno);
+			xioctl(fd, VT_DISALLOCATE, (void*)(ptrdiff_t)vtno);
 		}
 	}
 	return EXIT_SUCCESS;
